  <p>
    Influenza (the flu) is a contagious respiratory illness caused by influenza viruses. Symptoms typically
    appear suddenly — often described as feeling like you've been "hit by a truck." Understanding the common
    symptoms helps you recognize the flu early and take appropriate action.<a href="#ref-1" class="citation">[1]</a><a href="#ref-2" class="citation">[2]</a>
  </p>

  <h2>How Long Do Flu Symptoms Typically Last?</h2>
  <p>
    For most healthy adults, the worst flu symptoms last <strong>3-5 days</strong>, with full recovery taking
    <strong>1-2 weeks</strong>. However, cough and fatigue can persist for several weeks after other symptoms
    resolve. The most intense period — with high fever, severe body aches, and extreme fatigue — typically
    peaks around day 2-4 of the illness.<a href="#ref-1" class="citation">[1]</a><a href="#ref-2" class="citation">[2]</a>
  </p>

  <h2>Hallmark Symptoms of Influenza</h2>

  <h3>🤒 Fever and Chills</h3>
  <p>
    <strong>Fever is present in most flu cases</strong>, typically ranging from <strong>100°F to 104°F (38°C to 40°C)</strong>.
    Fever usually lasts 3-4 days. Chills often accompany the fever — your body's way of generating heat to fight the virus.<a href="#ref-1" class="citation">[1]</a><a href="#ref-3" class="citation">[3]</a>
  </p>
  <div class="callout-info">
    <strong>Note:</strong> Not everyone with the flu develops a fever. It's less common in older adults and very young children.<a href="#ref-1" class="citation">[1]</a>
  </div>

  <h3>😮‍💨 Cough</h3>
  <p>
    A <strong>dry, persistent cough</strong> is one of the most common flu symptoms. It can last for
    <strong>2 weeks or more</strong> after other symptoms resolve. The cough is caused by inflammation
    in the airways. If your cough produces colored mucus or worsens after initial improvement,
    it may indicate a secondary bacterial infection.<a href="#ref-1" class="citation">[1]</a><a href="#ref-4" class="citation">[4]</a>
  </p>

  <h3>😣 Body Aches and Muscle Pain</h3>
  <p>
    <strong>Severe muscle and body aches</strong> are a hallmark of influenza. Unlike a cold, where aches are mild,
    flu-related myalgia can be intense — affecting the back, arms, legs, and even making your skin feel tender.
    These aches are caused by the immune system's inflammatory response and typically improve within 3-5 days.<a href="#ref-1" class="citation">[1]</a><a href="#ref-5" class="citation">[5]</a>
  </p>

  <h3>😴 Extreme Fatigue</h3>
  <p>
    Flu fatigue is <strong>not ordinary tiredness</strong>. It's a profound exhaustion that can make even
    getting out of bed difficult. Fatigue often begins before other symptoms, peaks during the illness,
    and can persist for <strong>2-3 weeks after recovery</strong>.<a href="#ref-2" class="citation">[2]</a><a href="#ref-3" class="citation">[3]</a>
  </p>

  <h3>😩 Headache</h3>
  <p>
    Severe headaches are common with the flu, often felt <strong>around and behind the eyes</strong>.
    The headache is typically more intense than what you'd experience with a common cold and may worsen
    with eye movement or bright lights.<a href="#ref-1" class="citation">[1]</a><a href="#ref-4" class="citation">[4]</a>
  </p>

  <h3>🗣️ Sore Throat</h3>
  <p>
    A sore throat is common in the early stages, though it's usually less prominent than the cough and
    body aches. It typically improves within a few days as other symptoms intensify.<a href="#ref-1" class="citation">[1]</a>
  </p>

  <h3>👃 Nasal Congestion</h3>
  <p>
    Runny or stuffy nose is common but tends to be <strong>less severe than with a cold</strong>.
    It may appear after the initial onset of fever and body aches.<a href="#ref-3" class="citation">[3]</a>
  </p>

  <h2>Less Common Symptoms</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Gastrointestinal symptoms:</strong> Nausea, vomiting, and diarrhea are less common in adults but more frequent in children<a href="#ref-1" class="citation">[1]</a><a href="#ref-2" class="citation">[2]</a></li>
    <li><strong>Eye symptoms:</strong> Redness, watering, light sensitivity<a href="#ref-4" class="citation">[4]</a></li>
    <li><strong>Dizziness:</strong> Can occur, especially when dehydrated<a href="#ref-3" class="citation">[3]</a></li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Symptom Onset Pattern</h2>
  <p>Flu symptoms typically appear <strong>suddenly</strong> (unlike a cold, which develops gradually):<a href="#ref-1" class="citation">[1]</a></p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Hour 0-12:</strong> Sudden fatigue, mild body aches, possibly a scratchy throat</li>
    <li><strong>Hour 12-24:</strong> Fever develops, body aches intensify, headache begins</li>
    <li><strong>Day 2-4:</strong> Peak symptoms — high fever, severe aches, dry cough, extreme fatigue</li>
    <li><strong>Day 5-7:</strong> Fever breaks, cough may intensify, fatigue persists</li>
    <li><strong>Week 2+:</strong> Gradual improvement, lingering cough and fatigue possible</li>
  </ol>

  <div class="callout-warning">
    <strong>When symptoms differ:</strong> If you experience sudden shortness of breath, chest pain, confusion,
    or your fever returns after improving, seek medical attention — these may indicate complications.<a href="#ref-1" class="citation">[1]</a><a href="#ref-5" class="citation">[5]</a>
  </div>

